    <statement><p>Consider the planes 
<m>3\!x + 3\!y + 1\!z = 1</m> and 
<m>3\!x + 1\!z = 0.</m></p>
<p>(A) Find the unique point P on the y-axis which is on both planes.
(<fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>, <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0002"/>, <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/>)</p>
<p>(B) Find a unit vector <m>\bf{u}</m> with positive first coordinate that
is parallel to both planes.</p>
<p><fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0004"/> <m>\mathbf{i}</m> +
<fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0005"/> <m>\mathbf{j}</m> +
<fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0006"/> <m>\mathbf{k}</m></p>
<p>(C) Use parts (A) and (B) to find a vector equation for the line of
intersection of the two planes,<m>\bf{r(t) =}</m></p>
<p><fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0007"/> <m>\bf{i}</m> +
<fillin characters="15" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0007_1"/> <m>\bf{j}</m> +
<fillin characters="15" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0007_2"/> <m>\bf{k}</m></p>
<p><alert> NOTE: </alert> Part (C) is counted as a single question. <alert> All three </alert> components must be filled in to receive credit.</p>
<p>There are <em> many </em> different correct answers to this question.</p>
</statement>
